{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the design and most of the code satisfy the requirement. One part of the implementation is marked only with other requirements, and that is a gap.\n\n**What is covered**\n- **Pinned grammars:** `Cargo.toml` pins `tree-sitter` and all eight grammar crates with exact `=` versions.\n- **Profile selection:** `profile_for_path` picks the profile by extension and falls back to the plain-text profile. `profiles()` lists Rust, Python, TypeScript, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML.\n- **Profile tables:** each language has a `KindRule` table in `src/outline/{rust,python,typescript,css,markdown,toml,yaml}.rs`.\n  - `NameRule` expresses named versus anonymous nodes and the field or child that supplies the name.\n  - `Profile.attaches` expresses attached siblings: Rust attributes and outer doc comments, and Python decorators.\n- **Plain text:** `plain_text` produces one anonymous `line` node per non-blank line.\n- **Comments and transparency:** `Builder::walk` records comments without making them nodes. It descends transparently through unmapped grammar nodes. The root node comes from `root_node` and covers the whole file. No profile defines a fallback kind.\n- **Documentation:** `md/reference.md` has an \"Outlines\" section with a profile table and profile details. It says upgrades are deliberate changes.\n- **Tests:** `src/outline/tests.rs` and `src/authoring/tools.rs` cover the main cases.\n  - Python (decorator and comment range, unmapped top-level code belongs to the root), TypeScript and TSX, CSS, Markdown (including front matter and HTML comments), TOML and YAML.\n  - Plain-text lines.\n  - Parse-error reporting.\n  - The root owning unmapped code.\n  - No fallback kinds.\n  - The `outline` tool end to end.\n\n**Gap: incomplete `{impl}` marking**\n- `Builder::finish` in `src/outline/mod.rs` (lines 493–599) builds the final outline tree from the drafts.\n  - It creates the root node and the `OutlineNode`s.\n  - It links parents and children.\n  - It computes each node's `line_start`/`line_end`, extending the start to the earliest attached sibling. The requirement says explicitly that attached siblings are part of a node for its line range.\n- That code is marked only `{impl}` `requirements.references.outline_selectors`. It has no `{impl}` for `requirements.references.outlines`, even though it implements part of this requirement.\n- The Rust `KINDS` table in `src/outline/rust.rs` is also marked only for `rust_item_selectors`. This is a smaller issue, because `PROFILE` carries the `outlines` mark.\n- Per the review rules, code that implements part of the requirement without an `{impl}` reference to it is a gap. The code is correct; the traceability is incomplete.\n\nI found no `FIXME` acknowledging this gap, so the determination is false rather than FIXME.",
